# A defined val's initializer is elaborated in the empty context and
# inlined at its use. Here it applies family F at A := T with T unbound
# (read as itself, as in family-unbound-need.moss), and is used inside
# `outer`, which assumes T, so the caller's environment gives T a meaning
# (Concrete). Audit evaluates the inlined code in the caller's environment,
# so the family's key mentions T while T means Concrete there (family2-STATUS,
# "inlined consts"). Oracle (moss_m2): accepted, runs, exit 0. Native:
# rejects the use of K ("no binding provides this item"), as it does every
# use of a defined val (even `val K: () = ();`), so it says nothing here.
# Rule B (AUDIT-CHANGES #60): now a rejection test. The call use_it()
# needs use_it's requirement T in force, and T is neither bound nor
# assumed where it is called (nor merged with anything in force), so the
# family demand described above never happens.
# Audit: rejects. Oracle: rejects ("calling `use_it` needs `T` in force
# here"). Native: accepts (exit 0) until it implements rule B.
type A;
type T;
tag Concrete;
assume A { fn .foo(); }
functor F[A]: A -> A.foo;
functor G: A -> A.foo { bind A.foo = () => (); }
fn run();
assume T, T.foo { fn use_it(x: ()) { } }
val K: () = { bind F = G; use_it({ bind run = () => (); run() }); };
assume T { fn outer() { let k = K; } }
fn main() {
  bind T = Concrete;
  outer();
}
